 "disclosure": "Responsible disclosure: the malicious code was already public in each upstream repository, so every still-infected upstream project received one focused public pull request that removes it, with a high-level description only. We do not publish the payload, attacker infrastructure or reproduction steps. Each project decides whether to accept its pull request; Sol.College has no partnership with these projects and makes no claim about their production systems.",
 "recommendations": [
  "Treat any machine that ran dev/build/lint/test in an affected repo, or opened it with automatic tasks allowed, as compromised and rotate wallet keys, GitHub/npm tokens, SSH keys and .env secrets.",
  "Disable automatic tasks in VS Code/Cursor (task.allowAutomaticTasks: off) and review .vscode/ in pull requests.",
  "Protect default branches, require reviews and signed commits; the injecting commits were unsigned and some imitated normal or 'security fix' commits."
 ],
 "notAudit": "This is an automated signature-based review, not a security audit. A clean scan is not a guarantee that a repository is safe."
}
